WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.157 --> 00:00:03.157
(suspenseful music)

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:07.600
- These are all of the
volcanoes in the world.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.030
You've got this big
strip here in Ethiopia,

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:11.930
a big cluster up in Iceland,

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:15.910
and a long strip down the
west coast of the Americas.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.320
But there's one spot
that has more volcanoes

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.620
than anywhere else on Earth,
right here in Indonesia.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.410
And it's not even all of Indonesia,

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:27.220
this country of 17,000 islands.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.760
It's really this belt right here.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.440
This is called the Sunda Arc.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.090
And it's where the majority
of volcanic activity

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.780
in Indonesia is happening.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.220
It's where the violence, the intensity,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.710
the beauty of all of these
blasts is taking place.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:44.930
I want to show you some of
the most striking volcanoes

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:46.520
on Earth, and I'm doing this

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.730
because I want to try to better understand

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.740
why they are so deadly
and why millions of people

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:55.788
live right in the shadow
of these sleeping dragons.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:58.788
(suspenseful music)

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.090
Okay. Let's quickly go
over why volcanoes exist.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.560
Maybe you've heard this
before, but I'm going to try

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.260
to explain it in a way that makes sense.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:15.800
It's important to remember that the ground

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.670
under your feet is actually a giant plate

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.460
a hundred kilometers thick,

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.010
one of many plates floating
around on molten lava.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.810
They're all moving in certain directions

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.330
and at different speeds.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:30.150
There are seven main plates on Earth

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.690
and a bunch of smaller ones.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.120
And they're always moving.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.900
When these plates move into
each other, they make mountains.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.350
When they spread apart, they
make underwater volcanoes

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.500
that cool and turn into rocks
and we call them islands.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.210
When they slide against each other

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:45.000
they make terrible earthquakes.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.210
And when they run into each other

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:48.600
and one gets forced below the other,

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.440
they make volcanoes.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.900
- [Narrator] A whole series
of volcanoes begins to pop up

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.700
along the edge of the continent,
parallel to the trench.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.210
- So the real reason why this
one strip of land in Indonesia

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:02.720
has so many volcanoes
is because this plate

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.760
is slamming into this plate at a rate

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.470
of seven centimeters a year,

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.370
which is one of the fastest movements

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:10.700
of plates in the world.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.650
So you're getting a ton of this,

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.114
which results in a ton of this.

00:02:15.114 --> 00:02:18.070
(exciting music)

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:19.610
If you zoom into one of these volcanoes,

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:22.090
you're going to see a
lot of this dark green

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:24.270
around the volcano where no one lives.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.880
But all of this, each
one of these little dots,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.610
are villages where tons of people live.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.100
And they're right in the
strike zone of this volcano.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.020
Volcanoes kill people
when they suddenly erupt,

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:37.080
but it's not the lava that kills them.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.373
It's the ash.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.770
This huge column of ash
and gases first goes up

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:46.300
and then it eventually falls down.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:50.010
As it falls it gains momentum
on these steep mountain sides.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.710
And eventually that
will slam into villages

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.810
up to three kilometers in
radius from the volcano.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.710
If you were standing in the
way of one of these massive

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.700
ash avalanches, you will die.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.840
And then if it rains, it gets really bad,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.740
because the ash and the
rain make these horrifying

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:09.090
mud rivers that cut through the mountain

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.463
and flood villages all around the region.

