00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.870
Every volcano has its own personality.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:20.010
And I want to show you some
of the most striking volcanoes

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:22.210
along this strip and in the process,

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:25.340
explain how and why humans live so close

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:27.522
to these sleeping dragons.

00:04:27.522 --> 00:04:30.850
(exciting music)

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:33.360
First, let's talk about Kawah Ijen.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:36.880
This volcano blew up
really bad 3,000 years ago,

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.480
and left a one kilometer
wide crater that is now full

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.500
of this bright blue, very acidic water.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.330
The most acidic lake on Earth,
because under the surface

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.210
there is a huge deposit of sulfuric gas

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.390
that is venting out of
this crack in the earth.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:54.270
All of this sulfur gas
means that when this thing

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.830
erupts with lava, it burns that gas

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:00.430
and ignites it in this
mesmerizing blue flame.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:02.100
All of this gas also means

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.300
that there is some money to be made.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.750
The locals have installed
these ceramic pipes

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:10.490
to catch the gases and force
it to condense into a liquid.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:12.950
It flows down here to where it cools

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:16.180
and turns into a solid which
can be harvested by locals.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:17.840
My friend Christian is there right now,

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:19.930
covering the realities of this job.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:21.160
I'm going to let him show you

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.283
what this work is actually like.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:25.853
- Hey, Johnny.

00:05:25.853 --> 00:05:30.537
So right now we are inside of
Kawah Ijen, a active volcano.

00:05:30.537 --> 00:05:33.370
And this crater is full of life.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:36.270
You can see all the steam
rising off of the water,

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.330
this insane plume of yellow smoke,

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:42.350
and this yellow smoke is
what creates the sulfur.

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:46.000
And with me here are the
bad-ass, hardworking dudes.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.310
No other way to put it.
They're literal superheroes..

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:50.900
- [Man] This here is
Supano. What's up Supano?

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:54.000
- I've had this amazing
opportunity to just pick his brain,

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.500
try to understand a little bit better

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.400
why is it that these guys are taking on

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.720
some of the most
challenging work conditions

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:01.890
on planet Earth.

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:05.010
What is the heaviest weight
that you'll take up the hill?

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:08.110
- 75 kilogram.
- 75 kilograms.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:10.790
I just tried 40. I could barely manage it.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:12.000
I mean, you look at these guys,

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.000
they're not bodybuilders,
but they're strong minded.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.820
They're incredibly driven

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:18.140
and they've got a good purpose for it.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:23.140
- How much money? For one kilo only 1,250.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:26.008
- [Christian] So how much do
you make at the end of the day?

00:06:26.008 --> 00:06:27.170
- 300,000.

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:28.650
- If you were to convert
that to you U.S. dollars

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:33.650
that's about 20 to $21 for the
entire day's worth of work.

00:06:34.151 --> 00:06:36.901
(exciting music)

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:39.970
- These workers make it into town

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:42.830
where they sell this sulfur to factories,

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:45.480
which mainly use it to bleach
sugar so that it's white.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:48.140
It's back-breaking, dangerous work.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:51.500
Christian made a whole
video from this mine

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:52.990
and the realities of this work.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:54.570
I will link to that in the description.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:58.250
Next up we have Mount
Merapi or Fire Mountain.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:02.110
This volcano has killed
more humans than any other.

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:05.550
It erupts all of the time,
like every few years.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:08.350
Here it is erupting just last year.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:11.270
This thing is happening over
the course of five minutes.

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:15.290
This time-lapse was recorded
by VolcanoYT and Frekom,

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:16.920
two different YouTube
channels that we'll link to

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:17.760
in the description.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:20.300
The reason why this volcano is so deadly

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:21.970
is because like I said before,

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:24.550
there are millions of
people living around it.

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:26.290
And not just remotely around it,

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:28.230
like right up against the mountain.

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:29.770
Here's the volcano.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:33.000
And these are all the
villages right up against it.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.100
Which raises an important question

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:36.730
I had going into this story,

00:07:36.730 --> 00:07:39.080
which is why do so many people live here?

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:40.810
Like so many people.

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:44.760
The answer is that this is
an amazing place to live.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.670
(upbeat music)

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.230
Indonesia has mild warm weather.

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:52.470
It rains super hard for a few months

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:55.670
and that rain is effectively
stored in these mountains.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:58.500
And it provides water to these
people throughout the year.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.570
And then there's the fact that
this is perfect fertilizer,

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:05.130
all mashed up and broken down,

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:07.930
ready to make this soil
incredibly fertile.

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:10.660
This is some of the most
fertile land on Earth.

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:14.440
You get three rice harvests
from this soil every year

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:16.540
because the soil is like magic.

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:18.130
So yeah, the people
living here are sort of

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:20.890
in a high risk, high reward situation.

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:22.730
They live on a sleeping dragon

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:24.450
that could wipe them out at any moment.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:26.020
And yet, as long as it doesn't,

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:27.440
they have some of the best weather

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.433
and growing conditions on Earth.

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:33.263
Next up is Krakatau.

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:36.020
What you're looking at here is actually

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:39.640
like a graveyard of what
used to be a big island.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:41.300
It used to look a lot more like this,

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:43.580
but a hundred years ago, it blew up.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:46.510
We think this is the
most explosive volcano

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:47.920
in modern history.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.450
10% of the world could hear
the blast, it is so intense.

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:54.010
Tens of thousands were killed in the ash

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:56.510
and the ash exploded so high into the air

00:08:56.510 --> 00:09:00.650
that all the way in Europe
the sky was red and hazy.

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:03.870
Which has led some to speculate
that it was this event

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:08.240
that inspired the red hazy
look of this painting,

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:09.870
one of the most famous
paintings in the world

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:11.420
called "The Scream".

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.170
(exciting music)

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:18.980
The volcano that grew out of
Krakatau has kept erupting

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:21.790
over the years, breaking
this island down further

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:25.330
and further until the whole
thing just eventually collapsed

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:28.250
and caused a huge tsunami
that killed hundreds.

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:30.090
Just a couple of years
ago, the island went

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:32.540
from looking like this to this.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.420
And today it's one of the fastest growing

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.060
volcanoes in the world, and will certainly

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:38.703
be back for more destruction.

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:42.460
Okay, the last one I want to talk about

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:44.330
is this set of volcanoes.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:48.550
This one is Mount Bromo and
this one is Mount Semeru.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:50.630
First off, these two
volcanoes are some of the most

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.510
stunning volcanoes I've
ever seen in my life,

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:56.450
but they also represent the
deep religious symbolism

00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:59.700
that volcanoes have come
to symbolize over time.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.590
This mountain, Mount
Semeru, is named after

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:04.190
the mythical Mount Meru,

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:06.800
which is a sacred five-peaked mountain

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:08.610
in the cosmology of three religions;

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.550
Hindu, Jain, and Buddhism.

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:13.230
It is considered to be
the center of the physical

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.750
and metaphysical and spiritual universes.

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:18.560
The neighboring volcano
is also deeply symbolic.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:21.210
It's called Bromo, which
in Javanese is the name

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:23.900
for Rama or the Hindu god of creation.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:26.600
Every year, Hindus hike up this mountain

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:30.640
and make an offering of
vegetables and plants and animals

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:33.686
to the gods embodied in this volcano.

00:10:33.686 --> 00:10:36.900
(gentle music)

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.730
There are so many more
volcanoes in Indonesia.

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:43.800
And my goal is to go to
Indonesia with my camera

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:46.070
and actually photograph them for myself.

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:49.120
A lot of the footage from this
video came from Christian,

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:50.250
who gave me a lot of his footage.

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:52.350
He lives in Indonesia
and from other places.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:54.020
I didn't go there, but I really want to.

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:56.160
I'm now deeply fascinated with volcanoes

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:59.090
because I've been deeply
diving into plate tectonics.

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:02.370
So I hope you understand more
about why volcanoes exist,

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:05.020
why so many exists in Indonesia,

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:08.100
and how the people cope living next to

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.870
these ticking time bombs.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:12.920
What's so intriguing to me is
that the volcano is a giver.

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:16.170
It gives abundance and
fertility most of the time.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:19.423
But sometimes it chooses to take away.

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:22.493
Okay. That's it.

